1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,290
Imagine if you could make $100 in an hour,

2
00:00:04,290 --> 00:00:07,410
or a $1,000 in an hour,

3
00:00:07,410 --> 00:00:09,435
which would you choose?

4
00:00:09,435 --> 00:00:13,605
I choose the $1,000 because for the same amount of time,

5
00:00:13,605 --> 00:00:15,360
I would make more money.

6
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:18,015
Similarly in product management,

7
00:00:18,015 --> 00:00:22,755
with limited resources, we want to build things that provide more value.

8
00:00:22,755 --> 00:00:24,645
As a product manager,

9
00:00:24,645 --> 00:00:29,535
you'll run into lots of different types of issues that you'll need to take care of.

10
00:00:29,535 --> 00:00:34,215
A couple of examples are new features that you want to build.

11
00:00:34,215 --> 00:00:36,570
Bugs that have been discovered,

12
00:00:36,570 --> 00:00:41,180
dog food feedback that identifies a problem with your product,

13
00:00:41,180 --> 00:00:45,680
outages and other items that are in your backlog.

14
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:48,620
These are all important types of issues that

15
00:00:48,620 --> 00:00:51,515
you'll need to address at some point in time.

16
00:00:51,515 --> 00:00:56,110
However, you likely won't be able to address everything at once.

17
00:00:56,110 --> 00:00:59,000
Because you can't address everything at once,

18
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,015
this is where priority comes into play.

19
00:01:02,015 --> 00:01:05,585
Priority is a measure of how important something is,

20
00:01:05,585 --> 00:01:08,705
like a feature, bug, issue or task.

21
00:01:08,705 --> 00:01:11,060
It's important to remember that priority is

22
00:01:11,060 --> 00:01:14,450
subjective and determined by the product manager.

23
00:01:14,450 --> 00:01:18,500
You should make sure to take stakeholder input into account,

24
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:22,460
as well as understand the impacted projects context.

25
00:01:22,460 --> 00:01:27,425
You can use prioritization to help determine where to focus your efforts.

26
00:01:27,425 --> 00:01:34,150
Prioritization forces you to make trade-offs of where you or the team should spend time.

27
00:01:34,150 --> 00:01:40,355
Good prioritization results in people spending time on the highest impact things.

28
00:01:40,355 --> 00:01:42,575
Since priority is subjective,

29
00:01:42,575 --> 00:01:47,615
let's walk through some attributes that help determine the priority of an issue.

30
00:01:47,615 --> 00:01:51,365
First, you'll want to understand how this issue

31
00:01:51,365 --> 00:01:55,235
impacts the ability to deliver user and business value.

32
00:01:55,235 --> 00:01:58,265
Imagine there's an issue with your app crashing.

33
00:01:58,265 --> 00:02:01,490
If the app crashes when trying to place an order,

34
00:02:01,490 --> 00:02:05,990
that's pretty bad and negatively impacts both user and business value.

35
00:02:05,990 --> 00:02:10,270
That issue should be fixed urgently and as soon as possible.

36
00:02:10,270 --> 00:02:16,145
Now, imagine that your app crashes when you're going about this app screen.

37
00:02:16,145 --> 00:02:19,790
This isn't great. Crashes are never a good thing.

38
00:02:19,790 --> 00:02:24,680
But this specific issue doesn't negatively impact user or business value.

39
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:26,855
It should still be fixed at some point,

40
00:02:26,855 --> 00:02:29,360
but the urgency is not the same.

41
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:33,425
Another thing to consider is reputational risk.

42
00:02:33,425 --> 00:02:38,995
Reputational risk is the damage that could occur if something didn't happen.

43
00:02:38,995 --> 00:02:41,530
For example, a bug that wasn't fixed,

44
00:02:41,530 --> 00:02:43,700
that had severe consequences.

45
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:46,850
Reputational risk is mostly subjective,

46
00:02:46,850 --> 00:02:49,385
but sometimes can be quantified as well.

47
00:02:49,385 --> 00:02:52,985
When internal stakeholders see a low severity issue as

48
00:02:52,985 --> 00:02:57,830
a reputational risk then the priority of that issue becomes higher.

49
00:02:57,830 --> 00:03:00,890
This is very common with issues around privacy,

50
00:03:00,890 --> 00:03:05,330
where a report of a user's data leaking or being compromised could be picked up by

51
00:03:05,330 --> 00:03:10,490
the media or go viral and it would damage the reputation of the company.

52
00:03:10,490 --> 00:03:16,550
Another example is poor capacity planning resulting in an unreliable service.

53
00:03:16,550 --> 00:03:18,200
A recent example being

54
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:23,585
an online ticketing platforms sale for a famous artists upcoming concert.

55
00:03:23,585 --> 00:03:26,810
Many users were unable to purchase tickets.

56
00:03:26,810 --> 00:03:29,075
They complained about it on social media.

57
00:03:29,075 --> 00:03:31,145
The mainstream news picked up on it,

58
00:03:31,145 --> 00:03:35,695
and the ticketing platform is now being investigated by the Justice Department.

59
00:03:35,695 --> 00:03:40,705
Frequency of occurrence of the issue also increases the priority of an issue.

60
00:03:40,705 --> 00:03:42,950
An issue with minor severity,

61
00:03:42,950 --> 00:03:49,700
but something that occurs frequently can be treated with a higher priority. For example.

62
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:51,880
an error message when saving a coupon.

63
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:55,955
If enough users run into this issue and you start to see

64
00:03:55,955 --> 00:04:00,215
a decrease in cereal sales because users believed the coupon won't be applied,

65
00:04:00,215 --> 00:04:03,220
the priority of this issue should increase.

66
00:04:03,220 --> 00:04:06,695
Lastly, it's important to isolate the impact.

67
00:04:06,695 --> 00:04:11,390
This is very important and different from the frequency of occurrence.

68
00:04:11,390 --> 00:04:13,520
Does it affect all users,

69
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:15,125
or just a subset?

70
00:04:15,125 --> 00:04:17,570
Does it affect all mobile and web,

71
00:04:17,570 --> 00:04:21,520
or just iOS or just iOS 11?

72
00:04:21,520 --> 00:04:24,680
Understanding the impact of the issue is extremely

73
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:29,160
important and can be used to increase or decrease the priority.

